# Line-ending policy — Tarjuman runs on Windows, Linux and a Raspberry Pi
# =============================================================================
# Without this file git kept reporting four files as "modified" immediately
# after a clean commit. The diff was 3398 insertions and 3398 deletions — the
# same count on both sides, with identical content. That is the signature of
# line-ending churn, not of a real change: Windows checks files out as CRLF,
# and every other machine then sees every line as rewritten.
#
# Left alone this gets worse, not better, once three machines share the repo:
# a laptop, a second PC and the Pi would each "modify" the dataset simply by
# checking it out, and the resulting conflict would be in a 4213-column CSV
# that nobody can resolve by hand.

# Normalise text to LF in the repository. Working copies still get native
# endings, so nothing changes for editors on Windows.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Datasets: never touched, never auto-merged.
#
# -text switches off end-of-line conversion entirely, which stops the churn at
# its source. It also makes git refuse to auto-merge these files, and that is
# deliberate: two machines recording samples in parallel produce appends that
# git would happily interleave into a silently corrupt dataset. A conflict you
# must resolve on purpose is far safer than a merge you never noticed.
*.csv -text
data/labels.json -text

# Generated artefacts — same reasoning, different origin. These are rebuilt by
# `npm run train` / `npm run export3d`, so a merge is never the right answer.
tarjuman/public/trained_signs.json -text

# Genuinely binary: mark them so git never inspects or converts them.
*.onnx  binary
*.pkl   binary
*.glb   binary
*.gif   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.ico   binary
*.mp3   binary
*.pdf   binary
